{"content_hash": "1803c53379181fc6", "version_id": 410, "article_id": 41, "level_code": "ja_n1", "lang": "ja", "level": 5, "level_label": "N1 上級", "title": "トランプ移民政策、社会保障給付を年2152ドル削減へ　報告書", "title_zh": "特朗普移民政策或让美国人每年少拿2152美元社保", "has_translation": true, "topic": "business", "source": "guardian_business", "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/09/trump-immigration-cost-social-security", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1485745492261-a3819c494d11?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8NHx8dHJ1bXAlMjBpbW1pZ3JhbnQlMjBwdXJnZXxlbnwwfDB8fHwxNzkxNTk0MzA3fDA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Nitish Meena on Unsplash", "author": "Nitish Meena", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@nitishm?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/signage-on-night-IFh4o-U-BGg?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(167, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(205, 46%, 48%)", "label": "BUSINESS", "seed": "863ffcf86722"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-09", "word_count": 310, "reading_minutes": 1.0, "lead": "トランプ政権の移民排斥策が続けば、2034年以降、高齢者の社会保障給付が平均で年2152ドル、率にして8.6％減る——最新報告書が試算した。移民労働者の急減は食料品や住宅の価格高騰、雇用喪失にも波及し、経済全体への打撃は長期化する恐れがある。", "lead_zh": "一份最新报告估算，如果特朗普政府的反移民政策继续下去，到2034年以后，老年人平均每年社保金将减少2152美元，降幅达8.6%。移民劳动力骤减还推高了食品和住房价格，并导致就业流失，整体经济冲击可能长期化。", "preview": "移民政策の経済的影響を分析した報告書「トランプ政権移民政策の経済的影響」は、移民排斥策の帰結として、2034年以降、高齢者が受け取る社会保障給付が平均で年2152ドル、8.6％減ると予測する。過去2年間で外国生まれの労働者120万人が急減し、食料品価格の上昇や住宅建設許可の減少、", "preview_zh": "这份名为《特朗普政府移民政策的经济影响》的报告预测，反移民政策的后果是，到2034年以后，老年人领取的社保金平均每年减少2152美元，降幅8.6%。报告称，过去两年外国出生劳动力骤减120万人，导致食品价格上涨、住宅建设许可减少，甚至让美国本土出生的人也丢掉工作。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 400, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/41/ja_n1.mp3", "duration": 78.375, "size_bytes": 474048, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 214, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@0a373f3/content/data/versions/410.json", "paragraphs": ["移民政策の経済的影響を分析した報告書「トランプ政権移民政策の経済的影響」は、移民排斥策の帰結として、2034年以降、高齢者が受け取る社会保障給付が平均で年2152ドル、8.6％減ると予測する。過去2年間で外国生まれの労働者120万人が急減し、食料品価格の上昇や住宅建設許可の減少、さらには米国生まれの労働者までもが職を失う事態を招いているという。", "農業分野では、作物労働者の42％が不法滞在者、68％が外国生まれであり、多くの農家が労働力の大半、時には全員を一夜にして失ったと警告する。移民労働に依存する食料品は、2025年1月のトランプ再就任前の19カ月と比べ、牛乳5.7％、缶詰野菜6.3％、リンゴ7.2％と、食料品全体の平均を上回る値上がりを見せた。", "建設業でも、外国生まれの労働者が全職種の約3分の1を占める。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["这份名为《特朗普政府移民政策的经济影响》的报告预测，反移民政策的后果是，到2034年以后，老年人领取的社保金平均每年减少2152美元，降幅8.6%。报告称，过去两年外国出生劳动力骤减120万人，导致食品价格上涨、住宅建设许可减少，甚至让美国本土出生的人也丢掉工作。", "在农业领域，作物工人中42%是无证移民，68%是外国出生，许多农场主警告说，他们的大半甚至全部劳动力一夜之间消失。依赖移民劳动的食品，与2025年1月特朗普重新上任前的19个月相比，牛奶涨5.7%、罐装蔬菜涨6.3%、苹果涨7.2%，涨幅都超过食品整体平均水平。", "建筑业也一样，外国出生工人约占全部职业的三分之一。在依赖移民程度较高的州，2026年新建独栋住宅价格较2024年同期暴涨10.9%，从平均30万5752美元升至33万8752美元。2025年1月以来，全国独栋住宅建设许可减少10.6%，移民依赖度高的地区跌幅更大。"], "body": "移民政策の経済的影響を分析した報告書「トランプ政権移民政策の経済的影響」は、移民排斥策の帰結として、2034年以降、高齢者が受け取る社会保障給付が平均で年2152ドル、8.6％減ると予測する。過去2年間で外国生まれの労働者120万人が急減し、食料品価格の上昇や住宅建設許可の減少、さらには米国生まれの労働者までもが職を失う事態を招いているという。\n\n農業分野では、作物労働者の42％が不法滞在者、68％が外国生まれであり、多くの農家が労働力の大半、時には全員を一夜にして失ったと警告する。移民労働に依存する食料品は、2025年1月のトランプ再就任前の19カ月と比べ、牛乳5.7％、缶詰野菜6.3％、リンゴ7.2％と、食料品全体の平均を上回る値上がりを見せた。\n\n建設業でも、外国生まれの労働者が全職種の約3分の1を占める。", "body_zh": "这份名为《特朗普政府移民政策的经济影响》的报告预测，反移民政策的后果是，到2034年以后，老年人领取的社保金平均每年减少2152美元，降幅8.6%。报告称，过去两年外国出生劳动力骤减120万人，导致食品价格上涨、住宅建设许可减少，甚至让美国本土出生的人也丢掉工作。\n\n在农业领域，作物工人中42%是无证移民，68%是外国出生，许多农场主警告说，他们的大半甚至全部劳动力一夜之间消失。依赖移民劳动的食品，与2025年1月特朗普重新上任前的19个月相比，牛奶涨5.7%、罐装蔬菜涨6.3%、苹果涨7.2%，涨幅都超过食品整体平均水平。\n\n建筑业也一样，外国出生工人约占全部职业的三分之一。在依赖移民程度较高的州，2026年新建独栋住宅价格较2024年同期暴涨10.9%，从平均30万5752美元升至33万8752美元。2025年1月以来，全国独栋住宅建设许可减少10.6%，移民依赖度高的地区跌幅更大。", "text": "移民政策の経済的影響を分析した報告書「トランプ政権移民政策の経済的影響」は、移民排斥策の帰結として、2034年以降、高齢者が受け取る社会保障給付が平均で年2152ドル、8.6％減ると予測する。過去2年間で外国生まれの労働者120万人が急減し、食料品価格の上昇や住宅建設許可の減少、さらには米国生まれの労働者までもが職を失う事態を招いているという。 農業分野では、作物労働者の42％が不法滞在者、68％が外国生まれであり、多くの農家が労働力の大半、時には全員を一夜にして失ったと警告する。移民労働に依存する食料品は、2025年1月のトランプ再就任前の19カ月と比べ、牛乳5.7％、缶詰野菜6.3％、リンゴ7.2％と、食料品全体の平均を上回る値上がりを見せた。 建設業でも、外国生まれの労働者が全職種の約3分の1を占める。", "vocab": [{"word": "排斥", "pos": "noun", "zh": "排斥、排挤", "note": "移民排斥策＝排斥移民的政策"}, {"word": "給付", "pos": "noun", "zh": "给付、发放（福利金）", "note": "社会保障給付＝社保给付"}, {"word": "試算", "pos": "noun", "zh": "估算、试算", "note": "常与「する」连用"}, {"word": "急減", "pos": "noun", "zh": "骤减", "note": "与「急増」相对"}, {"word": "依存", "pos": "noun", "zh": "依赖", "note": "移民依存度が高い＝对移民依赖度高"}, {"word": "帰結", "pos": "noun", "zh": "结果、归结", "note": "较正式，多用于负面结果"}, {"word": "不法滞在", "pos": "noun", "zh": "非法滞留", "note": "不法滞在者＝无证移民"}, {"word": "値上がり", "pos": "noun", "zh": "涨价", "note": "物价上涨的常用说法"}, {"word": "急騰", "pos": "noun", "zh": "暴涨", "note": "比「値上がり」更剧烈"}, {"word": "人手不足", "pos": "noun", "zh": "人手不足", "note": "经济新闻高频词"}, {"word": "鈍化", "pos": "noun", "zh": "放缓、钝化", "note": "成長の鈍化＝增长放缓"}, {"word": "推計", "pos": "noun", "zh": "推算、估计", "note": "比「推定」更常用于统计"}, {"word": "歳入", "pos": "noun", "zh": "财政收入", "note": "此处指税收收入，文中用「給与税」"}, {"word": "財政", "pos": "noun", "zh": "财政", "note": "年金財政＝养老金财政"}, {"word": "受託者", "pos": "noun", "zh": "受托人", "note": "社会保障受託者＝社保受托人"}, {"word": "懸念", "pos": "noun", "zh": "担忧、顾虑", "note": "常用「懸念される」表示令人担忧"}], "title_original": "Trump immigrant purge could cut social security for Americans by $2,000 a year", "summary_original": "New report on economic cost of anti-immigration campaign projects 8.6% reduction in retirement payments by 2034\nElderly Americans can expect to lose an average of $2,152 in annual social security benefits each, a projected 8.6% reduction in their retirement payments, by 2034 and every year after as a direct consequence of the economic fallout from the Trump administration’s\nanti-immigration campaign\n, if that carries on, according to a new report shared exclusively with the Guardian.\nAlready, US food prices are up, housing permits to build new homes are down – and even native-born Americans are losing out on potential jobs after the sudden exodus of 1.2 million foreign-born workers over the last two years as a result of Donald Trump’s policies, the report finds.\nContinue reading...", "body_original": "Trump immigrant purge could cut social security for Americans by $2,000 a year\nNew report on economic cost of anti-immigration campaign projects 8.6% reduction in retirement payments by 2034\nElderly Americans can expect to lose an average of $2,152 in annual social security benefits each, a projected 8.6% reduction in their retirement payments, by 2034 and every year after as a direct consequence of the economic fallout from the Trump administration’s\nanti-immigration campaign\n, if that carries on, according to a new report shared exclusively with the Guardian.\nAlready, US food prices are up, housing permits to build new homes are down – and even native-born Americans are losing out on potential jobs after the sudden exodus of 1.2 million foreign-born workers over the last two years as a result of Donald Trump’s policies, the report finds.\n“When the Trump administration says things, it’s not always data-driven. And we wanted to see what the data shows, and what other people have found the data shows,” said Michael Ettlinger, one of the report’s authors. “Turns out that refutes what the US president primarily is asserting.”\nThe blowback on the economy from Donald Trump’s crackdown is wide-ranging, even as the federal government’s\naggressive\nmass deportation campaign\nquietly\nramps up\nfurther\n.\n“We are just beginning to see the harms in terms of what is happening here domestically. But the impacts of these policies, of these efforts, are going to be long-lasting. And I think that is by design,” Vanessa Cárdenas, executive director of the pro-immigration reform organization America’s Voice, told the Guardian.\nThe report, Economic Impacts of Trump Administration Immigration Policy, was commissioned by America’s Voice and conducted by researchers at Economic Insights and Research Consulting, a firm focused on economic analysis.\nIts conclusions underscore how recent federal\npolicies revoking\nimmigrants’ legal protections or targeting them for enforcement have had dire effects on the nation’s affordability crisis and job market, especially in regions and industries that rely disproportionately on foreign-born workers.\nIn the US agricultural sector, where 42% of crop workers are undocumented and 68% are foreign-born,\nfarmers\nacross the country are warning that the vast majority\nor in some cases all\nof their workforce has disappeared overnight, leaving produce to fall to, or rot in, the ground and go to\nwaste\n.\nFood products heavily reliant on immigrant labor have experienced much sharper price increases than food overall since Trump retook the White House, according to the report.\nExamples of immigrant-reliant food price increases that have exceeded the average increase in grocery prices over the last 19 months, compared with the 19 months before Trump returned to office in January 2025, are: fresh whole milk up by 5.7%, canned vegetables up by 6.3%, and apples up by 7.2%, the report found.\nIn that period before Trump’s inauguration, the vast majority of foods that principally depend on immigrant labor for production saw prices rise more slowly compared with other foods and some prices even fell outright, according to the report.\n“One thing that changed was the [immigration] policy. Before, the trend for all those goods was a lower inflation rate. And afterward, the trend was higher inflation. So the trend was reversed, and that was quite striking,” said Robert Lynch, one of the report’s authors.\nThe construction industry, where foreign-born workers fill almost a third of all occupations, has also been severely affected. States more dependent on immigrant labor have seen costs for new single-family homes soar 10.9% in 2026, compared with the first eight months of 2024, bringing building projects’ monetary value\nfrom an average of $305,752 to $338,752.\nAnd the issuance of housing permits to build single-family homes has dropped 10.6% nationwide since January 2025, with higher declines in regions more reliant on foreign-born workers, the research found.\nMeanwhile, native-born Americans are missing out on potential income in hard-hit states like California, Nevada, New Jersey, Florida and New York, where construction employment is on the decline – because when there are no immigrant roofers to build a home, American electricians and plumbers lose that project, too.\n“It’s not like someone can go work in construction and just not have any skills. And we’re losing people who have been here for decades working in construction. That could be fixed, but it’s going to take time,” Ettlinger said.\nOther industries with soaring costs and severe labor shortages include\nhealthcare\n, including home health care, nursing homes, adult daycare, lawn care, landscaping and groundskeeping, where immigrants have historically comprised a disproportionate share of the workforce and expertise.\nBy September 2025, less than nine months into the second Trump administration, native-born workers had already lost an estimated 51,000 to 297,000 jobs because of changes to US immigration policy, the report said. The labor force shrunk by an estimated\n919,000 people\nduring the 18 months of Trump’s second term, while\njob growth\nhas slowed to roughly a third of the rate it was before he retook office.\nThe federal government risks losing a large segment of the $26bn in payroll taxes from unauthorized immigrants who help to support the US social security fund that pays out to American seniors – while not being able to access those benefits themselves. Now, social security trustees,\nincluding\nthe treasury secretary, Scott Bessent, health and human services secretary, Robert F Kennedy Jr, labor secretary, Keith Sonderling, and social security commissioner, Frank Bisignano, are warning that lower net immigration than expected is threatening worse finances for the flailing retirement benefits system, which could mean cuts to Americans’ entitlements.\n“The share of that annual loss due to the Trump immigration policy would be $2,152 [per person on average]. Thus, the Trump immigration policies, if continued, would reduce projected average annual Social Security benefits by 8.6% in 2034, and every year thereafter,” the report’s authors write.\nThey told the Guardian that if the administration’s immigration agenda continues as expected, the implications for the\nUS economy\ncould get even worse.\n“We would expect higher prices,” Lynch said. “We would expect more job losses, slower economic growth and the affordability crisis getting worse – partly because prices are higher, partly because people are losing their jobs and their incomes and they can’t afford as much.”\nExplore more on these topics\nUS immigration\nUS economy\nnews\nShare\nReuse this content", "fetched_at": "2026-10-10T01:05:06.221848", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "移民", "s": 0.163, "e": 0.475, "sm": 162, "em": 475, "cs": 0, "ce": 2, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "政策", "s": 0.487, "e": 0.988, "sm": 487, "em": 987, "cs": 2, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "の", "s": 1.0, "e": 1.188, "sm": 1000, "em": 1187, "cs": 4, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "経済的", "s": 1.212, "e": 1.975, "sm": 1212, "em": 1974, "cs": 5, "ce": 8, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "影響", "s": 1.988, "e": 2.425, "sm": 1987, "em": 2425, "cs": 8, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "を", "s": 2.438, "e": 2.538, "sm": 2437, "em": 2537, "cs": 10, "ce": 11, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "分析", "s": 2.55, "e": 3.05, "sm": 2550, "em": 3050, "cs": 11, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "し", "s": 3.062, "e": 3.175, "sm": 3062, "em": 3175, "cs": 13, "ce": 14, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "た", "s": 3.188, "e": 3.312, "sm": 3187, "em": 3312, "cs": 14, "ce": 15, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "報告書", "s": 3.325, "e": 4.088, "sm": 3325, "em": 4087, "cs": 15, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "「", "s": 4.088, "e": 4.312, "sm": 4087, "